;;; A relation of the program's own left unguessed because a module it
;;; imports for another name loads an obligation rule —
;;; regression `symmetric-import` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `std.algebra` imported for `symmetric`, which makes `near` symmetric,
;;; beside `likes`, a relation the program declares and nothing closes, over
;;; two things. The program names no obligation and activates none; the
;;; import loads `std.algebra`'s two obligation rules with the module. The
;;; control, `rung-symmetric-written.ein`, deletes the import and writes the
;;; one rule it used, `std.algebra`'s `symmetric`, in its place.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; No hypothesis rule, and no obligation activated at root, so the blind enumerator
;;; builds H: `(likes A B)`, `(likes B A)`, `(near A B)` and `(near B A)`.
;;; Nothing refutes any of them, and `symmetric` makes each `near` fact bring
;;; the other: R is 16 commitments and 8 states, the control's answer.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; No candidate, and R {∅}, one state: the loaded obligation rules took the
;;; ladder's second rung, which owed nothing and proposed nothing. The
;;; read-out named `near` as outside the universe and said nothing of
;;; `likes`, which no rule concludes and no query asks about. The second rung
;;; is taken today where an obligation is activated at root, and the answer is
;;; the one above.

(import std.algebra :symbols (symmetric))
(relation near Thing Thing :why "{?3} is near {?4}")
(relation likes Thing Thing :why "{?3} likes {?4}")
(thing A) (thing B)
(symmetric near)
(solve)
